From b0a5ed533c0f88ce600b50e68b7558f5ab1327f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Z User <z@container>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 17:23:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] MachInst typed ParamRef materialization: single-move
 param staging

Census follow-up from the wide-cond round: ParamRef(needs-code) was the
top actionable rejection class (47 of 585 corpus files at -O2, 11.7% of
all instructions are ParamRefs). Three of emit_param_ref_impl's four
cases are single moves that MachInst expresses exactly; lowering them
keeps the buffered run unsplit where the text path flushed around the
materialization.

Admission mirrors the text path instruction for instruction (the final
machine state is byte-identical in every admitted shape):

  - alloca-slot load -> dest GPR: one extending/plain load straight into
    the destination register (movsbq/movswq/movslq/movzbl/movzwl/movl/
    movq per the load type), replacing mov_load_for_type + store_rax_to's
    rax round trip (2 -> 1).
  - incoming ABI register -> dest GPR: same forms from the incoming
    register, each read preceded by the pinned
    '# LCCC_PARAM_ABI_READ <reg>' marker (Raw) -- pin_param_abi_reads
    runs on the final text, so the MachInst-flushed marker reaches it
    unchanged and pins the very next line, which is exactly the move.
    A 64-bit param whose destination IS the incoming register is already
    in place (no-op); narrower types stay on the text path because the
    emitter's size-blind self-move elision would drop the extending move.
  - incoming ABI register -> dest slot: one reg->slot store (2 -> 1) for
    the widths where the stored bytes are byte-identical to the text
    relay (64-bit types; I32/U32 into small slots).
  - stack-passed scalars: the incoming-argument area addressed through
    the same slot-offset formula (StackSlot renders rbp/rsp-mode
    identically to slot_ref), same form table.
  - mem-to-mem (slot source -> slot dest): admitted only for the
    byte-identical relay widths (I64/U64/Ptr into 8-byte slots, I32/U32
    into 4-byte slots) -- the emitter's existing rax relay.
  - i128/struct/F128 classes: the text path's default arm emits nothing
    for them (prologue staging is the whole story), so a no-op lowering
    is semantically identical and keeps the run unsplit.
  - dead destinations: store_rax_to early-outs on zero use counts; the
    typed path no-ops the same way.

Refusals keep precise census labels: ParamRef(unsafe-home) 14,
ParamRef(slot-width) 3, ParamRef(float-domain) 0 (was one opaque
ParamRef(needs-code) 47). Coverage 96.21% -> 96.52%.

Pre-stored parameters keep their priority over the alloca slot (the
text path's order), and the is_mi_unsafe_value umbrella guard excludes
accumulator/i128/vector/xmm-homed destinations exactly as the typed
calls do.
