From 120d39e1c869ecd6a9cfffe9f5fc32ff67274d4d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Z User <z@container>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 17:23:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] const-local i128 initializers: refuse the i64 cache
 unless bit-exact

MISCOMPILE found by the follow-up i128-family sweep: every const-
qualified __int128 local was folded through the i64-sized
const_local_values cache. The initializer entered via
IrConst::I128(v).to_i64() = v as i64 -- the LOW half -- and every later
read of the variable folded to that half:

  const unsigned __int128 c = ((unsigned __int128)3 << 100) | 3;
  (unsigned long long)(c >> 64)

folded to 3i64.wrapping_shr(64 & 63 == 0) == 3: the high half read the
LOW value and the shift amount masked to zero. Observable from -O0 up
(pre-pass IR already carried Const(I64(3)) for both printf varargs);
GCC prints the full high half (3 << 36).

Fix: cache a 128-bit initializer only when the cached i64 IS the whole
value (non-negative and equal to the full constant through BOTH
widenings -- the reader widens by its own context, so sign- or
zero-extension must agree). Everything else stays uncached and reads
take the (correct) alloca path: a missed fold, never a wrong one.
Values <= 63 bits keep the existing fold behavior unchanged.

Tests:
- tests/regression/i128_const_local_half.c (new): mixed/high-only/
  all-ones/top-bit/ones-lo32 boundary values plus the shift-amount
  masking trap; byte-identical to gcc -O0/-O1/-O2/-O3/-Os.
- tests/regression/i128_pair_store_load.c: the const shape's high-half
  expectation was authored as 1 << 4 -- the value of a DIFFERENT shift
  (1 << 68); the C-semantics answer for (3 << 100) | 3 is 3 << 36 =
  206158430208 (GCC-verified). The self-check could never pass as
  committed; the corpus run had marked it FAIL since the merge.
---
